.TH TEE 1
.SH NAME
tee \- pipe fitting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tee
[
.B \-i
] [
.B \-a
]
[ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tee
transcribes the standard input to the standard
output and makes copies in the
.I files.
Option
.B \-i
ignores interrupts;
option
.B \-a
causes the output to be appended to the
.I files
rather than overwriting them.
.PP
.B Note:
a bare
.B \-
is accepted as a synonym for
.BR \-i .
That is v7's and its page did not mention it.
.PP
.B Note:
.I tee
copies bytes and inspects none of them, so a UTF\-8 file goes through it unchanged.
.SH "HOW MANY FILES"
.B Note:
at most
.B 19
files may be named \- one short of this system's limit of 20 open files per process, the
twentieth descriptor being the standard output
.I tee
always writes to.
A twentieth name is refused with
.B "tee: too many output files"
and the copy proceeds to the outputs already open.
.PP
v7 sized the same table at twenty and then wrote past the end of it, into the three variables
that happened to follow, so a twentieth file corrupted the program rather than being refused.
.SH "BUFFERING"
.B Note:
the buffer is one
.B 3072-byte
filesystem block, where v7's was 512 bytes \- the PDP\-11's block, which names nothing on this
machine.
.PP
When any output is a terminal or a pipe,
.I tee
writes what it has as soon as the input pauses, and in 16-byte pieces, so that an interactive
pipeline echoes as it is typed rather than a block at a time.
That is v7's and the number is unchanged.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A file that cannot be created is reported and dropped from the output list; the copy
continues to the rest.
The exit status is 0 either way, which is v7's.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), sh(1)
